;; WHAT EVERY FLINT ARTIFACT EXPOSES, AS DATA. `DECISIONS.md#four-operations` is
;; the prose -- a slug that outlived the fourth operation -- and
;; `bin/check-artifact-ops` is the script that checks each face against this.
;;
;; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Faces designed in parallel is how this project
;; produced sixteen version declarations and four compile-spec front ends. The
;; CLR face is the only one that exists today, so an assertion written now covers
;; one target -- and that is the point: the second face is checked the moment it
;; appears, against this, rather than against the first face by whoever remembers
;; to look.
;;
;; It lives under `runtimes/` rather than beside any one port because it belongs
;; to none of them. `runtimes/clr`, `runtimes/jvm` and the wasm target all answer
;; to it.
;;
;; THE FILE IS COUNT-NEUTRAL ON PURPOSE. It was `four-ops` for a day; `prop` was
;; then removed and everything named for the number had to be renamed. The count
;; lives in the data now, not in a filename.

{:format 2

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- the three
 ;;
 ;; `:spelling` is per target and deliberately NOT forced to match. Wasm cannot
 ;; pass an object or a callback, so its bridge is the host satisfying imports; a
 ;; CLR or JVM host hands over a real object. Forcing one spelling on all three
 ;; would be the mistake. The SEMANTICS are not negotiable.
 ;;
 ;; `:llvm` SPELLS THEM AS WASM DOES, and that is not laziness: both are flat C
 ;; symbol tables with no way to pass an object, so the same constraint produces
 ;; the same shape. It has NO FACE YET -- `:to :llvm` emits a module whose only
 ;; entry is `main` -- and the spelling is recorded now so the gate can name what
 ;; is missing rather than omit the target from its table.
 :operations
 [{:op "boot"
   :takes "the bridge, which becomes this sandbox's system port"
   ;; A FACTORY. What comes back IS the sandbox, which is what lets a host hold
   ;; more than one; see `loop` below for what the static version cost.
   :returns "the sandbox, on any target that can pass an object"
   :must "fail when a native the image imports cannot be supplied, naming it"
   :spelling {:clr "Boot" :jvm "boot" :wasm "flint_boot" :llvm "flint_boot"}}
  {:op "loop"
   :takes "nothing"
   :returns "a status; see :status below"
   ;; AN INSTANCE METHOD on the CLR and the JVM: `boot` answers a sandbox and
   ;; `loop` is that sandbox's. A static `loop` means ONE SANDBOX PER LOAD
   ;; CONTEXT, silently -- a second sandbox's pump would drive the first one's
   ;; scheduler with no error anywhere. Both object targets reached this
   ;; independently, which is better evidence than either arguing for it.
   :on "the sandbox boot answers, not the artifact's own type"
   :spelling {:clr "Loop" :jvm "loop" :wasm "flint_loop" :llvm "flint_loop"}}
  {:op "link"
   :takes "the bridge, a name and a function, overriding the native called that.
           THE BRIDGE, because link must precede boot and the image loads inside
           boot -- so there is no sandbox to hang it on yet, and the bridge is the
           sandbox's identity before the sandbox exists. Hooks are keyed per
           bridge, so two sandboxes can override the same native differently,
           which process-global registration could not do at all."
   :returns "nothing"
   :must "refuse after boot, because natives resolve exactly once at load"
   :spelling {:clr "Link" :jvm "link" :wasm "flint_link" :llvm "flint_link"}}]

 ;; `prop(name, buf)` WAS the fourth and is GONE (2026-09-24). Recorded here and
 ;; not merely deleted, because a face that grows it back would otherwise look
 ;; like an addition rather than a regression. The gate REFUSES it by name.
 :removed-operations
 [{:op "prop"
   :why "metadata must be readable WITHOUT executing the artifact, and a call is
         execution. All three containers already carry it; see :metadata below.
         The wasm face settled it: a module cannot read its own custom sections,
         so prop there needed a second copy of the metadata spliced into linear
         memory, a descriptor to find it, and ~100 lines of Rust scanning EDN
         with balanced-delimiter counting. Written, measured, reverted."
   :forbidden-spelling {:clr "Prop" :jvm "prop" :wasm "flint_prop"}}]

 ;; THE NUMBERS ARE THE ABI, AND ONLY THE NUMBERS ARE ASSERTED. They are
 ;; `flint_resume`'s and `rt.status`'s, unchanged, and this project treats them as
 ;; the contract itself rather than an implementation detail to be renamed per
 ;; host.
 ;;
 ;; The names below are the LOGICAL ones and the gate does not compare them. The
 ;; CLR spells them `enum Status { Done, Threw, NeedsHost }` and the JVM spells
 ;; them `static final int DONE, THREW, NEEDS_HOST`; an enum is how .NET spells
 ;; three named constants, and making either language write the other's spelling
 ;; would be forcing a divergence rather than fixing one. `:spellings` records what
 ;; each face actually writes, descriptively, so a reader can find it.
 :status {0 "Done" 1 "Threw" 2 "NeedsHost"}
 :status-spellings
 {:clr "enum Status { Done = 0, Threw = 1, NeedsHost = 2 }"
  :jvm "static final int DONE = 0, THREW = 1, NEEDS_HOST = 2"
  :wasm "the bare integers, as flint_resume already returned them"}

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- metadata
 ;;
 ;; Not an operation. Each container has a path the platform already understands,
 ;; and a reader gets at it without executing anything.
 :metadata
 {:value "pr-str of the map src/flint/modmeta.cljc builds -- ONE producer, one
          string, three containers, so the three cannot drift into different
          shapes"
  :carriers
  {:wasm {:kind "custom section" :name "com.3sln.flint.meta"
          :reader "host/modmeta.mjs"}
   :jvm {:kind "class attribute" :name "com._3sln.flint.Meta"
         :hazard "JVMS 4.7 REQUIRES an unrecognised attribute to be silently
                  ignored, so a collision here is QUIET"}
   :clr {:kind "CustomAttribute row" :name "_3sln.Flint.MetaAttribute"
         :reader "asm.GetCustomAttribute<_3sln.Flint.MetaAttribute>()"
         :evidence {:file "runtimes/clr/src/rt/MetaAttribute.cs"
                    :must-contain "namespace _3sln.Flint;"}}}
  ;; REVERSE DNS, EVERY ONE. All three are flat namespaces shared with everything
  ;; else in the process or toolchain. The wasm section was the bare word `flint`
  ;; until 2026-09-24. The gate asserts a dot in each name rather than trusting
  ;; that whoever adds the next one remembers.
  :names-must-contain "."}

 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------- rules
 ;;
 ;; Each is a sentence from the record that a face can get wrong silently, paired
 ;; with what the gate looks for.
 :rules
 [{:rule "link must precede boot, and refuse afterwards"
   :because "natives resolve exactly once, while the image loads, so a hook
             registered later is never reached and doing nothing quietly is the
             worst available answer"
   :evidence {:clr {:file "runtimes/clr/src/rt/Artifact.cs"
                    :must-contain "`Link` must be called before `Boot`"}}}
  {:rule "loop drains, drives, then drains again"
   :because "the scheduler reports NeedsHost while it holds undrained events, so
             a pump that skipped the trailing drain would report work it was
             itself holding"
   :evidence {:clr {:file "runtimes/clr/src/rt/Artifact.cs"
                    :must-contain "DRAIN, THEN RUN, THEN DRAIN"}}}
  {:rule "boot fails on a missing native, and names it"
   :because "the VERSION says what has to be linked, so a missing native is a
             version mismatch. This DEPARTS from Img.cs:138's deliberate
             null-tolerance and the trade is chosen: a version-matched host
             carries all 223 and nothing changes, a trimmed one now fails to boot
             a program it could have run"
   :evidence {:clr {:file "runtimes/clr/src/rt/Artifact.cs"
                    :must-contain "cannot supply"}}}
  {:rule "nothing decides what compatible means"
   :because "which semver relation counts is unsettled, and pre-1.0 makes the
             usual same-major rule say every release breaks everything"
   :evidence {:clr {:file "runtimes/clr/src/rt/Artifact.cs"
                    :must-not-match "(?i)\\bbool\\s+(is)?compatible"}}}
  {:rule "metadata is not reachable only by calling"
   :because "a runner decides WHETHER to load an artifact, and that decision
             cannot depend on having loaded it"
   :evidence {:clr {:file "runtimes/clr/src/rt/Artifact.cs"
                    :must-not-match "int\\s+Prop\\s*\\("}}}]}
